# Cloudflare reads this at deploy time, the same way it reads _headers.
#
# Both of these were live, indexed URLs for the web app service, which the site
# no longer offers: /mobile-app was the original, renamed to /web-app, and
# /web-app has now gone with the service. They point at /web-design, the nearest
# page that still exists — a redirect to the home page from a service URL reads
# as a soft 404 to a crawler, where this passes the standing on to something
# related.
#
# 301 rather than 302 on both: the moves are permanent.
/mobile-app  /web-design  301
/web-app     /web-design  301

# A dead URL was answering 404 with an empty body — no content-type, no
# CF-Cache-Status, nothing to render — so a mistyped address showed a blank
# white page instead of 404.html, which deploys fine and serves at /404.
#
# This hands Pages the file explicitly and keeps the 404 status, so crawlers
# still read it as gone rather than as a soft 404. It sits last because
# _redirects matches top-down and the two 301s above must win first; a splat
# does not shadow real files, which is the same reason an SPA fallback still
# serves its own CSS.
/*           /404.html    404
